<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-proofs-activity">

	<title>Module 6: What Properties Hold When?</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Proofs</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Rings</title>
		
    <p>
       Here's a reminder of the ring axioms which we will use in this module!
    </p>
    
    		<definition>
			<p>A <term>ring</term> is a set <m>R</m> together with two binary operations, called addition (<m>+</m>) and multiplication (<m>\cdot</m>), such that all of the following axioms hold:</p>
			
			<ul>
				<li><p><em>The set <m>R</m> is closed under addition and multiplication,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y \in R</m>, <m>x+y \in R</m> and <m>x \cdot y \in R</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Addition is associative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>(x+y)+z = x+(y+z)</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Addition is commutative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y \in R</m>, <m>x +y = y+x</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>The set <m>R</m> contains an additive identity,</em> also called a <em>zero element</em>, meaning that there exists some element <m>0_{R} \in R</m> such that <m>x+0_{R} = x</m> for all <m>x \in R</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Every element of <m>R</m> has an additive inverse within <m>R</m></em>, meaning that for every <m>x \in R</m>, there exists <m>y \in R</m> such that <m>x + y = 0_{R}</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Multiplication is associative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>(x \cdot y) \cdot z = x \cdot (y \cdot z)</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Multiplication distributes over addition,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>x \cdot (y+z) = x \cdot y + x \cdot z</m> and <m>(x+y) \cdot z = x \cdot z + y \cdot z</m>.</p></li>
			</ul>
			
		</definition>

    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<p>
    Here's a list of theorems.
    If we can prove these using ring axioms, we know they are true about all
    rings including - square matrices <m>\mathcal{M}_{n\times n}</m>, integers
    <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, even integers <m>\mathbb{E}</m>, the rational numbers
    <m>\mathbb{Q}</m>, polynomials <m>R[x]</m> for a commutative ring <m>R</m>,
    the real numbers <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, the complex numbers <m>\mathbb{C}</m>,
    and anything else we can show is a ring.
	</p>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Additive Cancellation) Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
            For all <m>x,y,z\in R</m>, if <m>x+z = y+z</m> then <m>x=y</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<theorem xml:id="proofs-thm-mult-0">
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Multiplication by <m>0</m> ) Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let
            <m>0</m> be the additive identity of <m>R</m>.
            Then <m>0x=0=x0</m> for all <m>x\in R</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Uniqueness) Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        (Additive identities) Suppose that both <m>0</m> and
                        <m>0'</m> are zero elements for <m>R</m>.
                        Then <m>0=0'</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        (Additive inverses) Let <m>x\in R</m>.
                        Suppose that both <m>y</m> and <m>y'</m> are additive
                        inverses for <m>x</m>.
                        Then <m>y=y'</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        (Multiplicative identities) Suppose that both <m>1</m>
                        and <m>1'</m> are both identities for <m>R</m>.
                        Then <m>1=1'</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        (Multiplicative inverses) Let <m>x\in R</m>.
                        Suppose that both <m>y</m> and <m>y'</m> are
                        multiplicative inverses for <m>x</m>.
                        Then <m>y=y'</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Fields are integral domains.) Let <m>R</m> be a ring with identity
            and let <m>x\in R</m> be a unit.
            Then <m>x</m> is not a zero divisor.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Finite integral domains are fields.) Let <m>R</m> be a finite
            integral domain.
            Let <m>a\in R</m> with <m>a\neq 0</m>.
            Then <m>a</m> is a unit.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
    In the following theorem note that the symbol <m>-x</m> means <q>the additive
    inverse of <m>x</m></q>.
    We define subtraction in terms of addition, e.g., <m>x-y = x + (-y)</m>.
</p>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (The negatives.) Let <m>R</m> be a ring, and let <m>x,y,z \in R</m>.
            Then
        </p>
       
       <sidebyside>
       	<ul>
			<li><p><m>-(-x) = x</m></p></li>
			<li><p><m>x(-y)=-(xy) = (-x)y</m></p></li>
			<li><p><m>(-x)(-y) = xy</m></p></li>
		</ul>
		<ul>
			<li><p><m>x(y-z) = xy-xz</m></p></li>
			<li><p><m>(y-z)x= yx-zx</m></p></li>
			<li><p><m>-(x+y)=-x-y</m></p></li>
		</ul>
       </sidebyside>

    </statement>
</theorem>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            (Generalized Distributive Law) Let <m>R</m> be a ring, let
            <m>n\ge 2</m>, and let <m>a,b_{1},b_{2},\dots, b_{n}\in R</m>.
            Then
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>a(b_{1}+b_{2}+\cdots+b_{n})=ab_{1}+ab_{2}+\cdots+ab_{n}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>(b_{1}+b_{2}+\cdots+b_{n})a = b_{1}a+b_{2}a+\cdots+b_{n}a.</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

	
	</page>
	
	<page>
		<paragraphs>
			<title>Let's prove some theorems!</title>
			
			<exercise workspace="2in">
			
				<p>In Weekly Practice 1 you proved in any field <m>F</m> and any <m>a\in F</m>, <m>0_{F}\cdot a = 0_{F}</m>. You actually only needed the ring axioms. Let's recall that proof.
</p>

<!-- Find a way to insert the original theorem here, OPEN, rather than having a duplicate theorem statement below -->
<!-- <xref ref="proofs-thm-mult-0" /> -->

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      (Multiplication by <m>0</m> ) Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>0</m> be
      the additive identity of <m>R</m>.
      Then <m>0x=0=x0</m> for all <m>x\in R</m>.
    </p>
<p>
  Prove it!
  Hint: Consider <m>(0+0)x</m> in two different ways.
</p>

  </statement>
</theorem>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<p> In Problem 5 of the proof portfolio, you'll prove
  additive and multiplicative cancellation holds in fields.
  For additive cancellation, you only need the ring axioms.
  Let's prove multiplicative cancellation holds in more than just fields.
</p>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      (Multiplicative Cancellation) Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>z</m> be a
      nonzero element of <m>R</m> that is not a zero divisor.
      For <m>x,y\in R</m>, if <m>xz=yz</m>, then <m>x=y</m>.
      Similarly if <m>zx = zy</m> then <m>x=y</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  Prove it!
  Hint: Starting with <m>xz=yz</m>, add <m>(-y)z</m> to both sides.
</p>
</exercise>
			
		</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	
<page>

<p>
  We established in the preview activity for this
  module that every field is an integral domain.
  It turns out every <em>finite</em> integral domain is a field.
</p>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      (Finite integral domains are fields.) Let <m>R</m> be a finite integral
      domain.
      Let <m>a\in R</m> with <m>a\neq 0</m>.
      Then <m>a</m> is a unit.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<p>
  Prove it!
  Okay, this one has a trick, so let's walk through some parts.
</p>

	<exercise workspace="1in">
      <p>
        Since <m>R</m> is an integral domain, <m>R</m> has identity.
        Consider the set <m>A</m>:
        <md>
          A := R- \{0\} = \{1, a_{1},a_{2},\dots, a_{n}\}.
        </md>
        Let <m>a\in R</m> with <m>a\neq 0</m>.
        Multiply each element of <m>R-\{0\}</m> by <m>a</m> to get
        <md>
          B := \{a,aa_{1},aa_{2},\dots, aa_{n}\}.
        </md>
        Explain why all elements of <m>B</m> are unique.
        (Go by contradiction - suppose for some <m>i,j</m>,
        <m>aa_{i} = aa_{j}</m>.
        Then what?
      </p>
     </exercise>


	<exercise workspace="1in">
      <p>
        Why does this imply that <m>a</m> has an inverse?
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        Give an example of an infinite integral domain that is not a field.
      </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        For which <m>n</m> is <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> an integral domain?
        For which <m>n</m> is <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> a field?
      </p>
    </exercise>

<p>
  You may read <m>-x</m> as <q>negative <m>x</m>.</q>
  When talking about rings, the symbol <m>-x</m> means <q>the additive inverse of
  <m>x</m>.</q>
  But the theorem labeled <q>the negatives</q> tells us that <m>-x</m> works like
  the negative we are familiar with.
</p>

<theorem>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>x,y\in R</m>.
      Then <m>x(-y) = -(xy)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
  Prove it!
  You want to show <q>the additive inverse of <m>xy</m> is <m>x(-y)</m>.</q>
  So we want to show <m>xy+x(-y)=0</m>.
  What ring axioms can you use to show this is true?
</p>
</exercise>

</page>



<page xml:id="sec-proofs-review">

<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>
      When does additive cancellation hold?
      When does multiplicative cancellation hold?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      How do we usually prove uniqueness?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      What is the relationship between fields and integral domains?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      What do we say instead of <q>negative</q> when reading <m>-x</m>?
      How do we prove things about <q>negatives</q>?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      How do we prove <m>0\times x = 0</m> for any ring <m>R</m> and
      <m>x\in R</m>?
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
        
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 6</title>


		<exercise number="1">
		<title>Matrix problems</title>
		<statement><p>
  Prove <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m> (two by two matrices with
  entries from the real numbers) has a zero divisor.
  What reason besides having zero divisors is
  <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m> not an integral domain?
		</p></statement>
    	</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>A negative result</title>
		<statement>
		<p>
  We showed (or assume we showed) that for a ring <m>R</m> and <m>x,y\in R</m>,
  we have <m>x(-y) = -(xy)=(-x)y</m>.
  Consider the following proof that <m>xy =(-x)(-y)</m>.
</p>

  <p>
    <em>Proof:</em> From <m>x(-y)=-(xy)=(-x)y</m> we obtain <m>(-x)(-y) = x[-(-y)] = xy</m>,
    since in any ring the inverse of the inverse of an element is the original
    element.
  </p>

<p>
  Fill in the details.
  Then use the result to prove that if <m>u</m> in <m>R</m> is a unit, then
  <m>-u</m> is also a unit.
</p>

		</statement>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Yay, induction!</title>
		<statement><p>
  Prove the generalized distributive law by induction.
  Let <m>R</m> be a ring, let <m>n\ge 2</m>, and let
  <m>a,b_{1},b_{2},\dots, b_{n}\in R</m>.
  Then <m>a(b_{1}+b_{2}+\cdots+b_{n})=ab_{1}+ab_{2}+\cdots+ab_{n}</m>.
	</p></statement>
		
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>

